					<div id=3D"mw-content-text" class=3D"mw-body-content"><div class=3D"mw=
-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang=3D"en" dir=3D"ltr"><table class=3D"plai=
nlinks messagebox mbox-side mbox-side-notice" role=3D"presentation"><tbody>=
<tr><td class=3D"mbox-image"><span typeof=3D"mw:File"><span><img alt=3D"" s=
rc=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Wikipedia-l=
ogo-v2.svg/60px-Wikipedia-logo-v2.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"45" =
height=3D"41" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"103" data-file-h=
eight=3D"94"></span></span></td><td class=3D"mbox-text"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/" class=3D"extiw" title=3D"w:">Wikipedia</a> has rela=
ted information at <a class=3D"external text" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Git_(software)"><i><b>Git (software)</b></i></a>.</td></tr></tbody=
></table>
<p>Instead of having to resort to a hosting company to store your central r=
epository, or to rely on a central server or internet connection to contrib=
ute changes to a project, it's quite possible to use removable memory to ex=
change and update local repositories.
</p><p>The basic steps are:
</p>
<ol><li>Mount the removable memory on a pre-determined path</li>
<li>Setup a bare repository in the removable memory</li>
<li>Add the repository in the removable memory as a remote</li></ol>
<p>Throughout this article, it's assumed that the repository will be mounte=
d in /media/repositories.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Starting_from_scratch">Star=
ting from scratch</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-edit=
section-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?ti=
tle=3DGit/Repository_on_a_USB_stick&amp;veaction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1" ti=
tle=3D"Edit section: Starting from scratch" class=3D"mw-editsection-visuale=
ditor"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-divider"> | </spa=
n><a href=3D"https://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=3DGit/Repository_on=
_a_USB_stick&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1" title=3D"Edit section's sou=
rce code: Starting from scratch"><span>edit source</span></a><span class=3D=
"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Let's assume a brand new project is being started, titled Foo.
The people working on project Foo will use a removable memory device as a c=
entral repository for the whole project.
For this reason, let's create a fresh repository in a USB stick. =20
</p><p>To start off, instead of relying on a path name generated automatica=
lly by the OS, the USB stick will be mounted on a custom path.
Let's assume the USB pen drive is located at /dev/sdb1, and the intended mo=
unt point is /media/repositories.
Another important aspect is that the USB pen drive needs to be mounted with=
 proper permissions, so that it doesn't cause problems within the repositor=
y.
One way to avoid these issues is to mount the USB drive with your own user =
ID and group ID, which can be achieved through the following commands:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>sudo<span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an>mkdir<span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories
$<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nv">my_uid</span><span class=3D"o=
">=3D</span><span class=3D"sb">`</span>id<span class=3D"w"> </span>-u<span =
class=3D"sb">`</span>
$<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nv">my_gid</span><span class=3D"o=
">=3D</span><span class=3D"sb">`</span>id<span class=3D"w"> </span>-g<span =
class=3D"sb">`</span>
$<span class=3D"w"> </span>mount<span class=3D"w"> </span>-o<span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"s2">"uid=3D</span><span class=3D"nv">$my_uid</spa=
n><span class=3D"s2">,gid=3D</span><span class=3D"nv">$my_gid</span><span c=
lass=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>/dev/sdb1<span class=3D"w"> <=
/span>/media/repositories
</pre></div>
<p>Having mounted the USB drive on the desired path, let's create a bare re=
pository.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nb">cd</span=
><span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories
$<span class=3D"w"> </span>mkdir<span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositori=
es/foo
$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </span>init<span class=3D"=
w"> </span>--bare<span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories/foo
Initialized<span class=3D"w"> </span>empty<span class=3D"w"> </span>Git<spa=
n class=3D"w"> </span>repository<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k"=
>in</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories/foo
</pre></div>
<p>With this step, a repository has been created in the USB memory drive. =
=20
Now, all that's left is to mount the USB pen on any computer on a specific =
path, clone the repository, and work away.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n>clone<span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories/foo
Cloning<span class=3D"w"> </span>into<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"s1">'foo'</span>...
warning:<span class=3D"w"> </span>You<span class=3D"w"> </span>appear<span =
class=3D"w"> </span>to<span class=3D"w"> </span>have<span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an>cloned<span class=3D"w"> </span>an<span class=3D"w"> </span>empty<span c=
lass=3D"w"> </span>repository.
<span class=3D"k">done</span>.
</pre></div>
<p>Done.
</p><p>In case the mount point changes to another path (in some cases, auto=
-mounting does that), the repository's URL can be set through the following=
 command:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n>remote<span class=3D"w"> </span>set-url<span class=3D"w"> </span>origin<s=
pan class=3D"w"> </span>file://&lt;path<span class=3D"w"> </span>to<span cl=
ass=3D"w"> </span>new<span class=3D"w"> </span>mount<span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an>point&gt;
</pre></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Pushing_local_repository_to=
_a_USB_stick">Pushing local repository to a USB stick</h2><span class=3D"mw=
-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=3DGit/Repository_on_a_USB_stick&amp=
;veaction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2" title=3D"Edit section: Pushing local repo=
sitory to a USB stick" class=3D"mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>edit</sp=
an></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=3DGit/Repository_on_a_USB_stick&amp;acti=
on=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2" title=3D"Edit section's source code: Pushing loc=
al repository to a USB stick"><span>edit source</span></a><span class=3D"mw=
-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Let's assume you've been working on a project, and you already have a wo=
rking git repository that you've been working on your desktop. =20
Now, you've decided that want to keep track and update several concurrent v=
ersions of that repository without having to use a network connection.  =20
One possible solution is to start a Git repository on a USB key, and use th=
at USB key as a centralized repository where everyone can push their contri=
butions and update their local version.
</p><p>To start a Git repository on a USB stick, first let's mount the USB =
stick on a selected path.
This is achieved through the process described in the previous section.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>sudo<span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an>mkdir<span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories
$<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nv">my_uid</span><span class=3D"o=
">=3D</span><span class=3D"sb">`</span>id<span class=3D"w"> </span>-u<span =
class=3D"sb">`</span>
$<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"nv">my_gid</span><span class=3D"o=
">=3D</span><span class=3D"sb">`</span>id<span class=3D"w"> </span>-g<span =
class=3D"sb">`</span>
$<span class=3D"w"> </span>mount<span class=3D"w"> </span>-o<span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"s2">"uid=3D</span><span class=3D"nv">$my_uid</spa=
n><span class=3D"s2">,gid=3D</span><span class=3D"nv">$my_gid</span><span c=
lass=3D"s2">"</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>/dev/sdb1<span class=3D"w"> <=
/span>/media/repositories
</pre></div>
<p>Let's assume that the project tree is located in ~/devel/foo.
To start a repository on /media/repositories/foo, run the following command=
:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>git<span class=3D"w"> </span>clone<span class=3D"w"> <=
/span>--bare<span class=3D"w"> </span>~/devel/foo<span class=3D"w"> </span>=
foo
</pre></div>
<p>That's it.
</p><p>Now, you can clone the Git repository stored in the USB drive and co=
ntinue working on your project.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n>clone<span class=3D"w"> </span>/media/repositories/foo/
Cloning<span class=3D"w"> </span>into<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"s1">'foo'</span>...
<span class=3D"k">done</span>.
</pre></div>
<p>If you wish to add to your local repository the newly created USB reposi=
tory as a remote repository,=20
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n>remote<span class=3D"w"> </span>add<span class=3D"w"> </span>usb<span cla=
ss=3D"w"> </span>file:///media/repositories/foo
</pre></div>
<p>To establish the master branch of the USB repository as the upstream bra=
nch of your local master branch, the contents of the newly added remote bra=
nch must be fetched and the upstream branch must be specified. =20
This step is performed by applying the following commands:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-bash mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"lt=
r"><pre><span></span>$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n>fetch<span class=3D"w"> </span>usb
From<span class=3D"w"> </span>file:///media/repositories/foo
<span class=3D"w"> </span>*<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">[</s=
pan>new<span class=3D"w"> </span>branch<span class=3D"o">]</span><span clas=
s=3D"w">      </span>master<span class=3D"w">     </span>-&gt;<span class=
=3D"w"> </span>usb/master
$<span class=3D"w"> </span>git<span class=3D"w"> </span>branch<span class=
=3D"w"> </span>--set-upstream-to<span class=3D"o">=3D</span>usb/master
Branch<span class=3D"w"> </span>master<span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"nb">set</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>up<span class=3D"w"> </span>to<=
span class=3D"w"> </span>track<span class=3D"w"> </span>remote<span class=
=3D"w"> </span>branch<span class=3D"w"> </span>master<span class=3D"w"> </s=
pan>from<span class=3D"w"> </span>usb.
</pre></div>
